     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · The Verge · 2026-10-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Amazon Warns Against Blocking AI Data Centers, Citing Economic and National Security Risks</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Amazon</span> <span>#AI</span> <span>#Data Centers</span> <span>#US Economy</span></div>
       <p>Amazon has issued a stark warning to communities considering blocking artificial intelligence (AI) data center projects, asserting that such actions risk irreparable harm to the US economy and national security. The call to action comes from Matt Garman, CEO of Amazon Web Services, who published a blog post exceeding 3,000 words today, directly addressing public concerns.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/amazon-warns-against-blocking-ai-data-centers-citing-economi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Garman’s blog pushed back against common criticisms regarding data centers' impact on jobs, power demands, and the environment. He emphasized the geopolitical importance of AI dominance, stating, “our nation can’t afford to lose” this crucial race. Garman also addressed the spread of “misinformation and outright lies” in the age of social media, citing reports of foreign countries intentionally seeding such information in the US to slow down data center development.</p><p>This robust defense by Amazon surfaces as public support for AI data centers has significantly declined in polls, facing increasing backlash from US communities where these facilities are being constructed. Widespread concerns over AI safety and its unchecked societal impact are also growing nationwide. Garman revealed that “over 100 data center moratoriums are being considered across the country,” warning that enacting these measures could lead the US to “writing its own losing ticket to this race,” with generational consequences.</p><p>In response to public scrutiny, Amazon is codifying several practices under a new “Data Center Commitment.” These include pledges to create jobs, prevent increases to local energy rates, and invest more than $1 billion to support communities adjacent to its data centers. Furthermore, Amazon announced it has ceased using nondisclosure agreements (NDAs) with government agencies, a move prompted by Democratic lawmaker Jamie Raskin's inquiry into tech companies' secret deals with local officials. David Zapolsky, Amazon chief global affairs and legal officer, commented in a Wall Street Journal interview that “the industry comes from a history of secrecy and so some of that’s just reflex,” acknowledging that community leaders require transparency for their constituents and that secrecy “just didn’t make sense” anymore.</p><p>What to watch: The impact of Amazon's commitment and public response to ongoing data center projects.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively synthesizes the source information, covering the blog content, the commitment, and the NDAs.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.theverge.com/tech/1003929/amazon-ai-data-center-blog-warning" rel="noopener">The Verge</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
