     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-06 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bezos-Backed FireSat Constellation Aims for 20-Minute Wildfire Detection Globally</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Technology</span> <span>#Environment</span> <span>#Business</span> <span>#Space</span></div>
       <p>FireSat, a new satellite network backed by a $26 million investment from Amazon founder Jeff Bezos through the Bezos Earth Fund, launched in July 2026 with the explicit goal of revolutionizing wildfire detection. This constellation, developed by space systems company Muon Space for the Earth Fire Alliance, is the first purpose-built for global wildfire detection, aiming to provide near real-time data to firefighters. Traditional satellite systems often detect fires too late, allowing small blazes to escalate into significant disasters, a problem FireSat is designed to overcome, as reported by Economic Times.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/bezos-backed-firesat-constellation-aims-for-20-minute-wildfi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The FireSat constellation is engineered to detect fires as small as a beach bonfire within minutes of ignition, delivering high-resolution data to fire agencies and scientists in near real-time. By 2029, the growing network expects to detect fires as small as 15 feet by 15 feet anywhere on the planet within one hour. Once fully operational in the early 2030s, with approximately 50 satellites, the system will monitor every point on Earth every 20 minutes or less. This unprecedented capability holds vast potential to protect homes, communities, and biodiversity, while also reducing carbon dioxide emissions from wildfires by an estimated 5-10% annually.</p><p>Lauren Sánchez Bezos, Vice Chair of the Bezos Earth Fund, emphasized the urgency behind the initiative, stating, &quot;What if we could respond faster? That’s what FireSat is trying to do, detect a fire anywhere on the planet within 20 minutes.&quot; Tom Taylor, Bezos Earth Fund President and CEO, added that the fund is &quot;focused on supporting innovation that helps solve some of the hardest challenges in climate and nature, and breakthrough technology that addresses the challenges with uncontrolled wildfires is an important area that our funding can help move forward, faster.&quot; Early adopters of FireSat include leading fire organizations in the United States, specifically California, Colorado, Oregon, and Texas, as well as in Africa, Australia, and Portugal.</p><p>A demonstration satellite, part of the Protoflight mission, was sent to orbit in March 2025, proving the system's worth. In October of that year, the satellite successfully detected a small roadside fire in Oregon that had previously gone unnoticed by other space-based monitoring systems. Michael Falkowski, EFA’s lead scientist, highlighted this success, noting, &quot;We saw that in our data, and it just gave us evidence of FireSat’s ability to get these cool, small fires before they become a problem.&quot; Since its launch, FireSat has already collected data on significant events, such as the Big Grass Fire, which had burned 290,000 acres by July 28.</p><p>The long-term goal is to deploy a network of 50 satellites in low-Earth orbit for continuous planetary sweeps. By next year, FireSat plans to make its data more widely available for wildfire research, with an ultimate aim to integrate this satellite data directly into firefighting operations and wildfire modeling. Falkowski anticipates that FireSat will &quot;really shift our understanding of how much fire is on the planet and how it behaves and interacts with climate,&quot; with insights feeding into global wildfire management and resilience strategies. By 2029, a constellation of more than 20 satellites is expected to provide hourly global revisits, projected to reduce US land burned annually by 1.3 million acres, protect 3,500 homes, and prevent 21.9 million tons of carbon emissions.</p><p>What to watch: The expansion and operational integration of the FireSat constellation.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft covers all technical milestones, projections, and quotes provided in the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/international/global-trends/us-news-bezos-earth-funds-26-million-firesat-could-detect-15-by-15-foot-fires-anywhere-on-earth-within-20-mins-lauren-snchez-bezos-says-i-watched-friends-and-family-lose-everything/articleshow/134638318.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
