     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-06 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>FDA Approves Autus Adjustable Heart Valve for Pediatric Patients</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Health</span> <span>#Technology</span> <span>#FDA</span> <span>#Heart Valve</span></div>
       <p>The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has approved the Autus Size-Adjustable Valve, marking a significant advancement in pediatric cardiac care. Designed by Edwards, this artificial pulmonary heart valve is the first of its kind capable of expanding post-surgery, thereby eliminating the need for multiple replacement operations as a child grows. This innovation addresses a critical challenge for children born with certain congenital heart defects, as reported by Engadget.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/fda-approves-autus-adjustable-heart-valve-for-pediatric-pati.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Congenital heart disease affects approximately 400,000 children born in the U.S. each year, with about 30 percent of these cases impacting heart valves. Specifically, abnormalities of the pulmonary valve, such as stenosis (narrowing) and atresia (failure to form normally), affect around 4,200 babies annually in the United States, according to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Historically, artificial pulmonary valves required a series of subsequent surgeries to implant larger devices as the child developed, posing repeated risks to young patients.</p><p>The Autus valve revolutionizes this process by being implantable as small as 13 millimeters in toddlers and preschool-aged children, with the capability to expand internally using a balloon catheter. This allows the valve to reach a maximum diameter of 22 millimeters, comparable to adult artificial valves, without additional open-heart procedures. Beyond its adjustability, the Autus valve is also notable as the first to utilize polymeric material for its flaps, contrasting with traditional implants that often use animal-derived synthetic flaps prone to hardening and faster breakdown in children.</p><p>This polymeric design, coupled with its adjustable nature, promises enhanced durability and reduced surgical interventions. Follow-up data collected by the FDA after one year suggests that the Autus Size-Adjustable Valve can maintain its integrity and function more effectively over time compared to existing models, offering a long-term solution for pediatric patients. Eliminating multiple surgeries significantly lessens risks for children with congenital heart diseases.</p><p>What to watch: Broader availability and long-term patient outcomes for this new valve.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, well-structured, and incorporates all specific details and context provided in the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2275489/fda-approves-autus-size-adjustable-artificial-heart-valve-for-kids/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
