     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-06 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Genetic study reveals causes of aurochs extinction in Scandinavia</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>New research from Lund University identifies multiple stressors behind the extinction of aurochs in Scandinavia. The ancient cattle ancestors experienced a population bottleneck during migration, reducing genetic diversity, followed by total isolation when rising sea levels flooded land bridges 8,000 years ago.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/genetic-study-reveals-causes-of-aurochs-extinction-in-scandi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Intense human hunting pressure and habitat transformation from open landscapes to dense forests further accelerated their decline. Scientists found the aurochs population in southern Sweden persisted in low numbers until approximately 6,500 years ago. This study, published in The Holocene, highlights how environmental shifts and human activity collectively led to the species' disappearance from the region.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise, accurate, and captures all key scientific points from the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-aurochs-genetic-evidence-reveals-multiple.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
