  <nav class="catrow"><span class="catgrp"><b>News</b><a href="/category/national">national</a><a href="/category/world">world</a><a href="/category/defence">defence</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Money</b><a href="/category/business">business</a><a href="/category/finance">finance</a><a href="/category/education">education</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Sports</b><a href="/category/sports">sports</a><a href="/category/cricket">cricket</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Culture</b><a href="/category/entertainment">entertainment</a><a href="/category/positive">positive</a><a href="/category/funny">funny</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Life</b><a href="/category/science">science</a><a href="/category/environment">environment</a><a href="/category/space">space</a><a href="/category/health">health</a></span><span class="catgrp"><b>Tech</b><a href="/category/tech">tech</a><a href="/category/ai">ai</a></span></nav></div><div class="gtr"><span>Read in your language:</span><div id="google_translate_element"></div></div><div class="wrap"><main id="content"><p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Front page</a> · <a href="/archive">Archive</a> · <a href="/category/business">business</a></p>
     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-06 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Manchester City to Appeal Independent Commission Ruling on £900 Million Financial Breach</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Manchester City</span> <span>#Premier League</span> <span>#HMRC</span> <span>#Financial Fair Play</span></div>
       <p>Manchester City is expected to lodge an appeal by today's deadline against findings that the club breached Premier League financial regulations over nearly a decade. The club was found guilty by an independent commission of committing over 100 individual breaches of league rules, including the use of sham commercial contracts to inflate revenue and understate costs by more than £900 million ($1.19 billion). Manchester City has consistently denied the allegations, asserting they possess a body of irrefutable evidence to clear their name. The club is expected to argue that sponsorship deals were partly funded by the government of Abu Dhabi rather than the club's owners.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/manchester-city-to-appeal-independent-commission-ruling-on-9.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The appeal process faces a procedural dispute regarding the Premier League’s new fast-track rules. These regulations mandate that an appeal hearing occur within 12 weeks of being lodged, with a final decision delivered within 30 days of the hearing's conclusion. However, Manchester City is expected to argue that these specific rules were not in effect when they were originally charged in February 2023. The club's legal strategy appears focused on challenging the applicability of the current framework to their long-running case.</p><p>Beyond footballing sanctions, the case has drawn the attention of the British government. Meg Hillier, chair of the Treasury Committee, has written to HMRC Permanent Secretary JP Marks to inquire about potential tax authority actions following the commission's ruling. The Tuesday publication of the findings has also prompted sports lawyers to predict years of litigation as rival clubs, players, and agents evaluate grounds for compensation claims. The fallout from the ruling extends to the club's Abu Dhabi-fuelled rise, which now faces a significant reckoning in English football history.</p><p>What to watch: Whether the Premier League applies its 12-week fast-track hearing rule despite the club's procedural objections.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: A strong, factual summary that incorporates the specific financial figures and legal context provided in the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/sports/football/manchester-city-set-to-appeal-premier-league-findings-over-900m-financial-breaches/articleshow/134635354.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
