       <h1>Pakistan Government and PTI Hold Talks to Ease Tensions</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#business</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Pakistani government and the Pakistan Tehreek-e-Insaf (PTI) party held negotiations on Friday to address political instability ahead of the opposition's planned long march to Islamabad on October 4. Facilitated by National Assembly Speaker Sardar Ayaz Sadiq, the talks involve a six-member government delegation and senior PTI leadership.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/pakistan-government-and-pti-hold-talks-to-ease-tensions.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>PTI is demanding access to jailed former prime minister Imran Khan for medical examinations, the release of arrested party members, and better treatment for Khan's wife, Bushra Bibi. Despite the ongoing dialogue, PTI officials confirmed that their call for the long march, scheduled to begin in Lakki Marwat, remains in effect.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief is concise and accurately summarizes the political situation and the specific demands of the parties involved, maintaining high factual density.</p>
