     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-06 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Psychological Criteria for AI Attachment Relationships Questioned</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Developmental psychologist Mary Ainsworth defined six conditions for an attachment relationship, including persistence over time, emotional significance, and seeking comfort from a specific, irreplaceable subject during distress. While AI companion apps promise close bonds, current research has not comprehensively tested these relationships against Ainsworth's criteria.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/psychological-criteria-for-ai-attachment-relationships-quest.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Recent surveys indicate that many users exhibit behaviors suggesting attachment, such as missing chatbots after separation. However, experts note that existing questionnaires often fail to measure the full scope of attachment, such as the need for security during threats. Consequently, whether human-AI bonds meet the formal psychological definition of attachment remains scientifically unproven.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief accurately synthesizes the psychological criteria for attachment and the current limitations of research regarding human-AI bonds.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-deep-ai-relationships-theory-expert.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
