     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Raza, Pooran, Ali Star on Canada Super 60 Day 3; Mississauga, Vancouver Secure Semi-Final Berths</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Canada Super 60</span> <span>#Sports</span></div>
       <p>Day 3 of Canada Super 60 Season 2, held on October 2, 2026, at BC Place, delivered compelling cricket action with Mississauga Masters, Toronto Sixers, and Vancouver Anchors all securing victories in the men's schedule. The day was highlighted by individual brilliance, including Sikandar Raza's rapid half-century, Nicholas Pooran's explosive 40 off 16 balls, and Moeen Ali's unbeaten 37 off 18 balls. Additionally, Vancouver Warriors Women claimed a win in their women's fixture, as reported by the Canada Super60 source.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/raza-pooran-ali-star-on-canada-super-60-day-3-mississauga-va.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Vancouver Anchors were the first men's side to win, defeating Brampton Blitz by six wickets. They bowled Brampton out for 84/9 in their 10 overs, with Lewis Gregory returning impressive figures of 4/13 from two overs, supported by Martin Andersson and Akhil Kumar, who each took two wickets. Anchors then chased the target comfortably, finishing on 90/4 in 8.4 overs. Moeen Ali led the reply with an unbeaten 37 from 18 balls, striking one four and four sixes at a strike rate of 205.56, while Obus Pienaar contributed 22 from 13 balls. Toronto Sixers followed with a five-wicket victory, chasing down White Rock Warriors' 128/6. White Rock's innings was built on Aryan Sidhu's 40 from 16 balls, including five sixes, and Dominic Drakes' unbeaten 38 from just 10 deliveries featuring six sixes. Pooran anchored Toronto's reply with 40 from 16 balls at a strike rate of 250, hitting five sixes, with Kieron Pollard adding 22 off nine balls and Daniel Sams finishing unbeaten on 27 off 10, including four sixes; Sams also contributed with the ball, taking 2/26.</p><p>Mississauga Masters produced the day's closest contest, posting 125/2 before holding Montreal Royal Tigers to 123/4 to win by two runs. Sikandar Raza was the key contributor with the bat for the Masters, reaching his fifty off just 24 balls as they set a total that Montreal could not quite overhaul. This result left Mississauga unbeaten after their opening two men's matches in the league phase. Earlier in the day, the women's fixture saw Vancouver Warriors Women beat Vancouver Anchors Women by eight wickets. Anchors Women posted 81/4 in 10 overs, with Fatima Sana Khan top-scoring on 49 from 31 balls, including five fours and three sixes. Sophie Reid then led the Warriors' chase with an unbeaten 37 from 16 balls, and Marizanne Kapp added 14 not out from seven, as Warriors reached 82/2 in 7.4 overs.</p><p>The strong performances on Day 3 contributed to subsequent developments in the tournament. According to Crictracker, Zaheer Khan co-owned Vancouver Anchors and Sikandar Raza-led Mississauga Masters both secured semi-final berths on Day 4 of Canada Super 60 Season 2. Mississauga Masters further solidified their dominance by finishing the league stage unbeaten on 10 points. In the women's competition, Toronto Sixers Women clinched their second successive title on Day 5, defeating Vancouver Warriors Women by six wickets, as also reported by Crictracker.</p><p>What to watch: The final stages of the Canada Super 60 Season 2.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, well-structured, and accurately reflects the provided source material and summary.</p>
