     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>South Africa and Namibia to Co-Host 2027 ODI World Cup Qualifiers</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#ODI World Cup</span> <span>#2027 Qualifiers</span> <span>#South Africa</span></div>
       <p>South Africa and Namibia are slated to co-host the 2027 ODI World Cup Qualifiers, a 10-team tournament scheduled from February 26 to March 21, 2027. The event's primary objective is to determine additional nations joining the main 2027 ODI World Cup. These dates were officially confirmed at ICC meetings in Dubai in late September 2026, revising an earlier window of February 22 to March 23. This revised schedule is strategically positioned between major domestic cricket events, with the SA20 league concluding on February 21, 2027, and the IPL 2027 season expected to begin in late March, running until May 30. For the main World Cup, South Africa and Zimbabwe have already secured automatic entry as co-hosts, alongside eight nations that qualified automatically based on ICC ODI rankings as of September 30, 2026: India, New Zealand, Pakistan, Australia, England, Sri Lanka, Afghanistan, and Bangladesh.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/south-africa-and-namibia-to-co-host-2027-odi-world-cup-quali.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The remaining berths for the global event will be contested through these qualifiers and a subsequent Super Series. Confirmed participants for the 10-team qualifier include West Indies and Ireland, with Ireland earning their spot as a Full Member based on their ODI rankings. They are joined by four nations from the top of World Cricket League Division 2: Scotland, USA, Netherlands, and Oman. A separate playoff, set for December 1 to 22 at an unannounced venue, will determine further participants. This playoff will involve the four bottom sides from WCL Division 2—Namibia, Canada, Nepal, and UAE—and the four highest-finishing Challenge League teams: Italy, Uganda, Jersey, and Kuwait. Namibia, as a co-host of the qualifiers, also has a unique alternative route to direct entry, requiring them to defeat USA and UAE twice each in matches starting October 3, provided other results involving Oman, Nepal, and Canada are favorable.</p><p>The qualification pathway from these tournaments to the 2027 World Cup is clearly defined. The winner of the 10-team qualifier will advance directly to the group stage of the 2027 ODI World Cup. Subsequently, the three remaining teams from the qualifier will compete in a Super Series, to be held in Namibia. The victor of this Super Series will also secure a place in the main group stage. Combined, the qualifier tournament and the Super Series will add two more nations to the 2027 ODI World Cup beyond the ten already confirmed, offering a structured pathway for associate nations and lower-ranked Full Members to participate in the global event. Specific match venues within South Africa and Namibia for the qualifier itself have not yet been confirmed.</p><p>What to watch: The outcomes of the December playoff and Namibia's attempts for direct qualification, influencing the final World Cup lineup.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately synthesizes the provided source information and organizes the complex qualification pathways clearly.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/2027-world-cup-qualifiers-to-be-held-in-south-africa-and-namibia-from-february-26-7050/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
