     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-10-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by The Hindu World, BBC World, News18 World">confirmed by 3 sources</span></div>
       <h1>Tennessee Prison Chief Resigns After Death Row Inmate Christa Pike Survives Execution Attempt</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Christa Pike</span> <span>#Tennessee</span> <span>#Lethal Injection</span> <span>#Death Penalty</span></div>
       <p>The head of Tennessee’s prison system has resigned following the failed execution of 50-year-old death row inmate Christa Pike on September 30, 2026. Pike, who was sentenced to death in 1996 for the killing of 19-year-old Colleen Slemmer, survived two doses of pentobarbital at the Riverbend Maximum Security Institution in Nashville. According to News18 and The Hindu, Pike remains in critical condition on a mechanical ventilator at a hospital. In response to the incident, Tennessee Governor Bill Lee has ordered a total pause on all executions for the remainder of the year and announced a third-party review into the state's lethal injection protocols.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/tennessee-prison-chief-resigns-after-death-row-inmate-christ.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Witnesses at the facility reported that the execution attempt lasted over an hour, during which staff struggled to establish an IV line. Pike’s lawyers stated that at least seven needles were used as authorities searched for a vein in her arm, with Pike eventually suggesting they try higher up. Despite the administration of pentobarbital—a drug typically used to treat seizures that slows brain activity—witnesses heard Pike snoring and breathing long after the doses were given. The curtains to the death chamber were closed multiple times, obscuring the view of witnesses who could only hear sounds from the procedure. Medical experts cited by The Hindu suspect the drug leaked into her tissues rather than entering the bloodstream.</p><p>This incident marks the second failed execution in Tennessee in 2026, following the called-off execution of Tony Carruthers earlier this year. The Death Penalty Information Center noted that while seven people have survived failed attempts due to IV issues, Pike is the first to survive after the actual injection of lethal doses. Her legal team has now requested a commutation of her death sentence, citing the 'degraded pentobarbital' and lack of emergency medical care during the botched procedure. Pike was 18 at the time of her 1995 crime, and her supporters have long argued that her history of childhood abuse and mental illness were not fully considered during her original sentencing.</p><p>What to watch: The findings of the third-party review ordered by Governor Bill Lee into the state's secret execution protocols.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the event, maintaining a neutral tone and accurately citing the sources.</p>
