     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-06 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tesla Rolls Out Emergency Drive-Away Feature After Supercharger Shooting</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tesla</span> <span>#Supercharger</span> <span>#Emergency</span> <span>#Safety</span></div>
       <p>Tesla has introduced a new &quot;Emergency Drive Away&quot; feature, enabling its vehicles to move while still connected to a Supercharger during urgent situations. This development comes months after a driver was shot inside a Tesla taxi at a Supercharger station located at an In-N-Out Burger in Twin Falls, Idaho. Previously, drivers needed to unlock the charge port via the vehicle's infotainment system, the Tesla app, or a button on the charger handle, a process deemed problematic for quick emergency exits.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/tesla-rolls-out-emergency-drive-away-feature-after-superchar.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Following the Idaho incident, Tesla owners advocated for a rapid escape system. The company announced the Emergency Drive Away feature on X, demonstrating its activation. If a driver shifts into Drive with the charger still attached, the infotainment system displays a notice to activate the override. It also warns that driving with the cable connected could damage both the car and the Supercharger station, explicitly stating, &quot;Use only in an emergency.&quot; To activate, users must hold the brake pedal and tap &quot;Activate&quot; on the screen.</p><p>Access to this new safety feature requires an upgrade to the latest software. Currently, it is exclusively available for Superchargers within the United States and does not support pre-2021 Model S and X vehicles. Tesla also stated on X that deliberate misuse &quot;will invoke additional penalties,&quot; clarifying that electrical and civil costs could amount to &quot;up to $25k&quot; if the feature is abused, though it remains unclear if Tesla would charge for activation in genuine emergencies.</p><p>What to watch: Monitor further details on potential charges for legitimate emergency use.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article effectively summarizes the new safety feature, its activation requirements, and the associated warnings regarding potential costs.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2275509/tesla-supercharger-emergency-drive-away-feature/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-06/tesla-rolls-out-emergency-drive-away-feature-after-superchar" data-title="Tesla Rolls Out Emergency Drive-Away Feature After Supercharger Shooting"><b>Share:</b>
