     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · News18 World · 2026-10-06 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge conf" title="Reported by News18 World, The Hindu World">confirmed by 2 sources</span></div>
       <h1>UAE Labels Flydubai Cockpit Incident 'Terrorist Act,' Praises Indian Pilot Smit Machchhar</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#UAE</span> <span>#Flydubai</span> <span>#Smit Machchhar</span> <span>#Terrorism</span></div>
       <p>The United Arab Emirates has officially described the attack aboard a Flydubai flight from Dubai to Tel Aviv as a “terrorist act,” as authorities continue their investigation into the incident and its motive. UAE presidential adviser Anwar Gargash made these remarks, commending Indian Captain Smit Machchhar for his actions during the mid-air emergency aboard Flydubai flight FZ1073.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/uae-labels-flydubai-cockpit-incident-terrorist-act-praises-i.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Gargash stated that Machchhar “deserves every appreciation” for confronting what he termed a serious terrorist act and a potential disaster, praising the pilot's “courage and responsibility.” He further described Machchhar as a “true hero,” highlighting his professionalism and moral conviction under extremely difficult circumstances, according to a post on X. Gargash also urged the public to avoid “simplistic interpretations” and the promotion of “conspiracy theories,” emphasizing the ongoing importance of vigilance against extremism and terrorism.</p><p>Two days prior, Flydubai flight FZ1073, en route from Dubai to Tel Aviv, was forced to divert to Saudi Arabia following an altercation in the cockpit. Saudi authorities reported that the captain was attacked by his co-pilot, resulting in injuries to both pilots. The aircraft subsequently landed safely at Tabuk Airport. During the incident, Captain Machchhar, despite his injuries, managed to open the cockpit door, allowing passengers and crew members to intervene and restrain the attacker. Other pilots on board then assisted in taking control of the aircraft. Machchhar was later transferred to the UAE for treatment and is currently recovering.</p><p>Following the incident, the UAE initiated a formal investigation to examine the circumstances and motive, including whether it involved “any terrorist activity or purpose” or “prior planning or direction.” The UAE has asserted its judicial authorities’ jurisdiction over the case, as the aircraft is registered in the country and operates under its flag. Concrete praise for Machchhar also came from Dubai Crown Prince, who expressed pride that the pilot is “part of our team… part of the story of our nation… and part of what the UAE stands for… a nation of compassion… a nation of peace… and a nation of life,” according to The Hindu World. Separately, Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu called Machchhar a “hero of the entire world,” as reported by The Hindu World. Public officials have urged against spreading unverified information while the probe continues.</p><p>What to watch: The ongoing investigation into the attack's motives and potential links to terrorism.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is comprehensive, covers all relevant details from the provided text, and accurately attributes the information.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>How this was reported · 2 sources</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.news18.com/world/uae-calls-flydubai-incident-terrorist-act-praises-indian-pilot-smit-machchhar-10364751.html" rel="noopener">News18 World</a></li><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/flydubai-attack-dubai-crown-prince-praises-indian-pilot-machchhar/article71540812.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>Synthesised from 2 independent reports on the same event. Details are attributed to the outlet that carried them; nothing outside these sources is published.</p>
    </div>
       
