     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-06 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Yuzvendra Chahal Retires from First-Class Cricket, Focuses on White-Ball Return</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Yuzvendra Chahal</span> <span>#First-class Cricket</span> <span>#White-ball Cricket</span></div>
       <p>Yuzvendra Chahal announced his retirement from first-class cricket on Friday, October 2, 2026, marking the end of a red-ball career that spanned 49 matches and yielded 148 wickets. The leg-spinner conveyed his decision via Instagram, clarifying that he is not stepping away from white-ball cricket and intends to actively pursue opportunities in both One-Day International (ODI) and T20 formats.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-06/yuzvendra-chahal-retires-from-first-class-cricket-focuses-on.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Chahal's final first-class appearance was for Northamptonshire in the County Championship Division Two, where he played against Middlesex at Lord's. This fixture concluded on September 26, 2026, and saw him claim two wickets. His retirement announcement followed a three-year stint with Northamptonshire, during which he represented the county in English domestic red-ball cricket.</p><p>Reflecting on his journey, Chahal wrote on Instagram, &quot;The first cricket ball I ever held was a red ball. I was 10 years old, and I had no idea then how deeply I would fall in love with this game.&quot; He elaborated, &quot;For me, red-ball cricket was never just another format. It was where I learned the meaning of patience, character, resilience and the pure joy of being out there for hours, fighting for every run and every wicket.&quot; He described playing his last First-Class match at Lord's as emotional, acknowledging that a Test cap was a long-held dream, though it didn't materialize. Despite this, he expressed contentment with his journey and gratitude for opportunities in the format, stating, &quot;The red ball gave me some of the most beautiful memories of my life, and nothing will ever take that away.&quot;</p><p>Chahal's first-class career statistics underscore his consistent wicket-taking ability, with 148 wickets from 49 matches. This record includes six five-wicket hauls, with his best bowling figures in an innings standing at 6/44. Prior to his county engagements, he represented Haryana in domestic red-ball cricket in India. In his retirement statement, he specifically acknowledged the Haryana Cricket Association, along with Mr. Ranbir Singh Mahindra and Mr. Anirudh Singh Chaudhary, for their continuous support and belief throughout his red-ball journey.</p><p>Looking ahead, Chahal made it clear that his retirement applies exclusively to first-class cricket. He articulated his enduring ambition, stating, &quot;But I AM NOT DONE YET. The hunger to play for my country is still very much alive. I believe I have the cricket left in me, and I will give everything I have to earn another opportunity in the One-Day and T20 formats.&quot; His most recent international appearance for India was in August 2023 against West Indies, and he has not represented the country at the international level since then.</p><p>Across 152 matches for India in ODIs and T20Is, Chahal has secured 217 wickets, establishing himself as one of India's more prolific white-ball spinners. He was also a member of India's squad that won the 2024 T20 World Cup. At 36 years old, Chahal's complete professional focus now shifts towards limited-overs cricket as he aims to enhance his international record.</p><p>What to watch: Chahal's efforts to secure a return to international white-ball cricket.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The article thoroughly utilizes the provided full text, maintaining accuracy and including specific dates, statistics, and quotes.</p>
